 the top 20-25% of starting hands\n- Against a random hand, A-10o wins roughly 60-65% of the time pre-flop\n\n**Position & Action:**\n- Opponent checked (called the big blind), showing no aggression\n- This suggests a weak-to-medium hand, or a trap with a premium hand (less likely given the check)\n- I'm in position to act after seeing their passive play\n\n**Strategic Considerations:**\n1. **Raise**: A standard raise (200-400 chips) would apply pressure and potentially take down the pot immediately. Given opponent's passive check, they might fold marginal hands. This also builds the pot when I likely have the better hand.\n\n2. **Check**: Free card to see the flop, but gives up the initiative and allows opponent to see the flop cheaply with any two cards. This is too passive given my reasonable hand strength.\n\n3. **All-in**: Extremely aggressive and unnecessary with 19,900 chips. This would only get called by better hands (pairs, AK, AQ) and fold out worse hands I'm dominating.\n\n4. **Fold**: Terrible option with A-10, which is well above average in heads-up play.\n\n**Best Move:** A standard raise of 200-400 chips to:\n- Build the pot with a likely superior hand\n- Gather information about opponent's hand strength\n- Potentially take down the pot immediately\n- Maintain flexibility for post-flop play\n\nReason: A-10 offsuit is strong enough in heads-up play to warrant aggression, especially against an opponent who showed weakness by just checking. A moderate raise of 200 chips accomplishes multiple goals: it applies pressure, builds value when ahead, and doesn't over-commit my stack. The opponent's passive check suggests they don't have a premium hand, making this an ideal spot to seize control of the pot.\n\nAnswer: 200"}
